Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/27/14-inch-macbook-pro-with-m6-chip/">today</a> replaced the entry-level 14-inch <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> with a new M6 model, bringing more CPU and GPU cores, upgraded wireless connectivity, and more. How much has changed, and is it worth upgrading or choosing the newer model?<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/Apple-MacBook-Pro-14-in-with-M6-hero-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="2560" height="1440" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1073586" /><br/>
<br/>
The M6 &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; retains the previous model's design and most of its specifications, concentrating its improvements on processing performance and connectivity. Both models offer 16GB of starting memory, 1TB of starting storage, and up to 24 hours of battery life.<br/>
<br/>
The M5 &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; originally launched at &#36;1,599 with 512GB of storage. Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/03/apple-m5-macbook-pro-storage-update/">removed that configuration in March</a>, making 1TB standard and increasing the starting price to &#36;1,699. A further <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/25/apple-just-increased-prices/">price increase in June</a> brought it to &#36;1,999 amid rising memory and storage costs. The M6 replaces that model at the same price.<br/>
