<h3 id="should_you_upgrade_from_the_m5_macbook_pro">Should You Upgrade From the M5 MacBook Pro?</h3><br/>
For existing M5 &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; owners, there is very little reason to upgrade. The M6 is a small refresh aimed primarily at buyers replacing a much older machine, with improvements to performance and connectivity that leave the overall experience essentially unchanged. It offers no increase in memory capacity, additional ports, or longer battery life. If the M5 model already handles your work comfortably, the newer model provides little practical incentive to replace it.<br/>
<br/>
If your current computer can last a little longer, it may also be worth waiting. A redesigned entry-level &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; with an M7 chip is <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/07/01/redesigned-m7-macbook-pro-2027/">reportedly planned for as early as the first half of 2027</a>, potentially offering a more substantial upgrade than the M6 refresh.<br/>
<br/>
<h3 id="should_you_buy_the_m5_or_m6_macbook_pro">Should You Buy the M5 or M6 MacBook Pro?</h3><br/>
For buyers replacing an Intel-based Mac or an older Apple silicon machine, the M5 and M6 deliver much the same experience. The more consequential improvements come from the hardware they share, so the price and configuration should carry more weight than the chip generation.<br/>
<br/>
<ul><br/>
 <li><a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/m5-vs-m6/">M5 vs. M6 Chip Buyer's Guide: How Much Better Really Is M6?</a></li><br/>
</ul><br/>
<br/>
At the same price, memory, storage, and condition, choose the M6. Its newer chip and wireless connectivity are welcome additions, but there is little reason to pay substantially more for them. A discounted M5 model is likely to be the better value for many buyers.<br/>
<br/>
Used and refurbished M5 models offer another potential saving: the 512GB configuration that Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/03/apple-m5-macbook-pro-storage-update/">discontinued in March</a>. If you do not need 1TB of internal storage, this version may provide a more affordable route to essentially the same &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; experience. The M6 starts at 1TB, leaving no equivalent lower-capacity option.<br/>
<br/>
